<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Carpathians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> are the section of the Carpathian mountains running through south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. They formed the western boundary of medieval Moldavia, separating it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. Their forested passes and valleys sheltered early Moldavian settlement, and the painted monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> lie in their foothills.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eastern-carpathians.jpg" alt="Eastern Carpathians" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:Markussep · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapcarpat2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
